@pablo-bastari-sw's manual JSON edit is the right unblock, and the underlying reason is that AutoGen 0.4+ moved to a component-config model where `tools` and `workbench` are mutually exclusive on `AssistantAgent`. Studio's drag-and-drop generates a config that has both populated, which is why you hit it even with the built-in tools.

the validator that rejects this lives on `AssistantAgent.__init__` in `autogen_agentchat/agents/_assistant_agent.py`. the rule is: a workbench (e.g. `McpWorkbench`, `StaticWorkbench`) owns tool dispatch end-to-end, so the agent must not also receive a separate `tools: [...]` list. otherwise there are two execution paths for the same call and the loader refuses rather than guess which wins.

picking which one to keep depends on what you want:

if you want plain python function tools (the old "skills" mental model from earlier studio versions), keep `tools: [...]` and remove the `workbench` key entirely:

```json
{
  "provider": "autogen_agentchat.agents.AssistantAgent",
  "config": {
    "name": "my_agent",
    "model_client": { ... },
    "tools": [
      { "provider": "autogen_core.tools.FunctionTool", "config": { ... } }
    ]
  }
}
```

if you want an MCP server or some structured toolset, drop `tools` and configure `workbench` only:

```json
{
  "config": {
    "workbench": {
      "provider": "autogen_ext.tools.mcp.McpWorkbench",
      "config": { "server_params": { ... } }
    }
  }
}
```

for studio 0.6.1 specifically, drag-and-drop a tool generates a `tools` entry but the parent agent template already has `workbench` baked in. easiest path: open the team JSON in the studio UI's "edit JSON" view, find the agent, delete the `workbench` block, save, restart the run.

if you want both plain-python tools AND an MCP workbench on the same agent, 0.4+ supports that by wrapping your python tools in a `StaticWorkbench` and composing workbenches, not by having both fields populated. inside one AssistantAgent the one-or-the-other rule still holds.

